  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness and prefers more right-skewed lotteries (i.e., those with higher probability on unfavorable outcomes). In this case, Lottery B is more right-skewed and would be preferred by the reason. However, the individual’s verbal report emphasizes wanting to win something with high probability, which reflects a preference for higher chance of non-zero outcomes — a consideration aligned with minimizing the chance of zero (i.e., favoring left-skew), opposite to the given reason. The individual considers the probability of winning something (non-zero), which is directly related to skewness but leads to the opposite preference. The individual values avoiding zero, while the reason values higher probability on unfavorable outcomes (i.e., zero). Thus, the individual’s reasoning contradicts the decision reason. The information considered (chance of non-zero win) is related but interpreted inversely, and the justification (favoring high chance of winning something) opposes the reason’s rule (favoring more right-skew). Therefore, it is certain that the individual did not use this decision reason.",
