  "step_2": "The individual states: 'Die Wahrscheinlichkeit etwas zu verlieren soll so klein wie möglich bleiben. Ob ich zu einer Wahrscheinlichkeit von 1/4 verliere oder 1/5 ist ein unterschied, dafür nehme ich auch das Risiko in Kauf mehr zu verlieren.' This translates to: 'The probability of losing something should remain as small as possible. Whether I lose with a probability of 1/4 or 1/5 makes a difference; for that, I am willing to take the risk of losing more.' The individual explicitly focuses on minimizing the probability of a loss, even if it means accepting a larger loss when it occurs. They recognize that 1/5 (20%) is smaller than 1/4 (25%) and prefer the lower chance of loss, despite the higher amount lost in that case. The justification is based on minimizing loss probability, not on the shape or skewness of the distribution. The individual acknowledges the trade-off between loss size and loss likelihood and chooses to prioritize reducing the likelihood.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on skewness and specifically prefers the more right-skewed lottery (higher probability on worse outcomes). In this case, Lottery A is more right-skewed (25% loss vs 20%), so the reason would prefer A. However, the individual expresses a preference for minimizing the probability of loss, which leads them to favor Lottery B (20% loss probability vs 25%). The individual is willing to accept a larger loss to reduce the chance of loss, indicating a preference for lower loss probability — the opposite of what the skewness reason prescribes. Moreover, the individual does not mention or consider the distribution shape, imbalance of probabilities across outcomes, or any concept related to skewness. They focus exclusively on the probability of incurring any loss and express a desire to minimize it. Therefore, the information considered (loss probability) does not align with the skewness-based information (distribution asymmetry), and the justification (minimize loss chance) contradicts the reason's rule (prefer more right-skewed). There is no evidence the individual used skewness in their reasoning.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
